  "result": "`Parameter.consume_value` (`src/click/core.py:2481`) checks four sources in order and stops at the first one that supplies a value. It returns a `(value, source)` tuple.\n\n1. **Command line.** It starts with `opts.get(self.name, UNSET)`, the value the parser produced. If that isn't `UNSET`, `source` is `ParameterSource.COMMANDLINE`. If it is `UNSET`, `source` starts as `DEFAULT`.\n2. **Environment variable.** If the value is still `UNSET`, it calls `value_from_envvar(ctx)`. A result that isn't `None` is used, and `source` becomes `ENVIRONMENT`.\n3. **default_map.** If the value is still `UNSET`, it calls `ctx.lookup_default(self.name)`. The result is used if it isn't `None`, or if `ctx._default_map_has(name)` says the key is present. That means an explicit `None` in the map still counts. `source` becomes `DEFAULT_MAP`. A string value for a parameter with `nargs != 1` is split with `type.split_envvar_value`, matching the environment-variable behavior.\n4. **Parameter default.** If the value is still `UNSET`, it calls `self.get_default(ctx)`. A result that isn't `UNSET` is used, and `source` is `DEFAULT`.\n\nIf nothing supplies a value, it returns `UNSET` with source `DEFAULT`.\n\n`Option.consume_value` (line 3589) wraps this. When the parser returns `FLAG_NEEDS_VALUE` (a flag used without a value), it resolves the sentinel in one of two ways:\n- **Prompt:** if the option has a prompt, it asks the user and sets `source` to `PROMPT`.\n- **Activation value:** otherwise it uses `flag_activation_value` and sets `source` to `COMMANDLINE`.",
